very_important.body:
Baroness Kidron's Amendment 172 would designate all NHS patient and public health data as a “sovereign data asset”. She used the EDITH AI breast-screening trial as an example, saying its secure cloud provider and software suppliers are US-owned and that data might be shared with “AI providers”, and warned against making UK health outcomes “more dependent on Silicon Valley”.

Lord Clement-Jones (LD) supported Amendments 143, 172 and 172C. He set out what sovereign status should mean:
- UK-only storage;
- no routine overseas support access;
- encryption keys held by NHS bodies rather than suppliers;
- national security checks on supplier ownership and governance, with NCSC advice;
- reporting to Parliament.
Citing the US CLOUD Act, he said “Storage location alone is not sovereignty”.

Lord Tarassenko (CB) moved Amendment 142 and spoke to 143 and to 172C on NHS AI adoption. Lord Patel (CB) said Scotland already keeps patient data on a UK-owned, encrypted platform. He called for a UK compute strategy “pretty soon” and said he hoped a Science and Technology Committee report on AI, genomics and personalised medicine would make similar points.

Lord Markham (Con) supported sovereignty over data but warned against turning it into “sovereignty over the technology”, meaning a requirement for UK-supplied technology. He argued that the best available technology should be used, and cautioned against using the amendments to exit the Palantir-run Federated Data Platform (FDP) contract, which he said has raised output by up to 10% in hospitals using it. Earl Howe stressed AI's productivity potential and the need for the UK to “remain self-reliant and secure”.

For the Government, Baroness Merron said:
- all 44 recommendations of the National Commission into the Regulation of AI in Healthcare have been accepted;
- health is among the first four focus areas of the sovereign AI R&D procurement scheme;
- an independent review of NHS productivity and AI reports early next year;
- she agreed with Baroness Kidron that building sovereign capability “does not mean that we have to do everything ourselves”.
She preferred an approach that evolves with the technology to fixed requirements in primary legislation. Lord Tarassenko asked leave to withdraw Amendment 142.

debates.body:
Peers supported a single patient record (SPR) in principle but repeatedly raised trust. Baroness Harding said earlier attempts to join up NHS data, including care.data, NPfIT and the Federated Data Platform, had failed to build citizen trust. Lord Patel and Lord Kakkar supported amendments to remove the power in Clause 52 for regulations to lift the common-law duty of confidentiality.

Other amendments sought:
- safeguards for sexual and reproductive health records and for psychotherapy notes;
- consistent definitions of anonymisation and pseudonymisation across the four nations (Baroness Harding urged the Information Commissioner to “get a grip” on this).
Lord Davies of Brixton also drew attention to an Office for Statistics Regulation report containing six lessons on the Federated Data Platform.

Baroness Merron said:
- the clause is a power, not a removal of patient–clinician confidentiality;
- there will be further engagement before Report;
- if the SPR were used for secondary purposes, the national data opt-out policy would apply;
- the Information Commission is consulting on draft guidance.
Lord Clement-Jones's closing contribution also recorded her commitment to issue and consult on draft regulations.

The committee considered the Consumer Subscription Contracts (Cancellation and Information) Regulations 2026 and the consequential amendments regulations, which implement the DMCCA subscription regime. It covers:
- clear information before sign-up;
- reminders;
- easy exit;
- cancellation rights, including cooling-off periods.
The Government estimates £436m a year of consumer benefit. Lord Hunt cited reported implementation costs of about £400m. Lord Clement-Jones supported the aims but questioned the timetable and workability. Lord Leong said there will be no enforcement grace period. The CMA will initially focus on supporting compliance, but conduct during that period may be considered in enforcement.

questions.body:
A cross-departmental series (46646, 46649, 46650, 46652, 46655, 46656, 46657, 46658, 46659, 46660, 46683) asks about use of open-weight AI models published by developers based in China.

Related questions ask:
- how many people from industry advise the Prime Minister's AI Taskforce (46514);
- how many full-time equivalent staff work in the GovAI Unit (46515);
- whether the centralised view of AI deployment will record which models each department uses (46681);
- whether HMT holds a record of AI models (46680);
- about AI finding weaknesses in cryptographic algorithms (46682).

In an earlier answer (902156), Sarah Owen (NIO) said the EU AI Act will apply in Northern Ireland only as a limited product-safety update, mainly for physical products with embedded AI, and will not cover use of AI, standalone AI software or general-purpose AI.

The series asks:
- whether ministers or senior officials have met Jensen Huang or other Nvidia representatives since 5 July 2024 (46777);
- about AI chip diversion to China and the potential merits of remote-access controls (46778);
- whether the Department for Business, Innovation, Science and Trade knew of other governments' concerns about the relationship between Jensen Huang and Megaspeed International before 29 October 2026 (46788);
- how far publicly funded AI compute, including Isambard-AI and the AI Research Resource, relies on Nvidia, the implications for sovereign AI, and contingency for depending on a single supplier (46795).

Jess Asato asked about the Meta AI / Muse assistant. The Government replied:
- AI services are in scope of the Online Safety Act where they meet its definitions of regulated user-to-user or search services;
- the Crime and Policing Act gives powers to bring unregulated chatbots into the illegal-content duties;
- it pointed to the DRCF letter on personal AI agents.
